 of the signal and you can  see both of those decrease over time but  if you look at the time interval it's  over years and when the the lines  intersect with the dashed lines that's  when the electrode is expected to be  completely ineffective so these data  were taken from original experiments in  monkeys and based on a lot of  observations over a thousand showing  that in fact you know we can get very  important data on the lifetime of these  electrodes and that we can show they  last for many years and in fact the  electrode that was in Kathy's brain  where she controlled that robotic arm to  drink her coffee was had been in place  for five years and was still functional  so we know we can make electrode arrays  that last for many years but of course  we really need them to last a lifetime  so they can always be functional  so there have been a number of  innovations recently although there's a  still a big transition phase to go from  animal work to human but one of them  that is most exciting to me is this  electrode that's being developed by I  mecha by Tim Harrisburg Duda and a  number of other collaborators this  electrode has on it more than 960 sites  so remember each probe on the electrodes  we use now has one these probes have 960  the digitizer can record 384 so we put  several of these electrodes in we could  record not a hundred neurons but 10,000  or more neuron the only problem is is  that this device is as you can see on  the left side is rather large it sticks  up out of the skin and so it will have  to be miniaturized more and have  electronics and the other problem is  that if we have thousands of channels  coming out the data rate is higher than  anyone can handle a radio cast  broadcasts that much information the  power demands are huge and we don't know  the safety profile so those are all  things that have to be overcome to make  an electrode this cannot can work all  the time in a in a human with a with  paralysis  now lastly the practical