 of the signal and you can  see both of those decrease over time but  if you look at the time interval it's  over years and when the the lines  intersect with the dashed lines that's  when the electrode is expected to be  completely ineffective so these data  were taken from original experiments in  monkeys and based on a lot of  observations over a thousand showing  that in fact you know we can get very  important data on the lifetime of these  electrodes and that we can show they  last for many years and in fact the  electrode that was in Kathy's brain  where she controlled that robotic arm to  drink her coffee was had been in place  for five years and was still functional  so we know we can make electrode arrays  that last for many years but of course  we really need them to last a lifetime  so they can always be functional  so there have been a number of  innovations recently although there's a  still a big transition phase to go from  animal work to human but one of them  that is most exciting to me is this  electrode that's being developed by I  mecha by Tim Harrisburg Duda and a  number of other collaborators this  electrode has on it more than 960 sites  so remember each probe on the electrodes  we use now has one these probes have 960  the digitizer can record 384 so we put  several of these electrodes in we could  record not a hundred neurons but 10,000  or more neuron the only problem is is  that this device is as you can see on  the left side is rather large it sticks  up out of the skin and so it will have  to be miniaturized more and have  electronics and the other problem is  that if we have thousands of channels  coming out the data rate is higher than  anyone can handle a radio cast  broadcasts that much information the  power demands are huge and we don't know  the safety profile so those are all  things that have to be overcome to make  an electrode this cannot can work all  the time in a in a human with a with  paralysis  now lastly the practical human system is  going to require something that can be  used at home allow the person to be  mobile and you can see Kathy sitting in  a real chair here and the yellow arrows  point out how there's a big cart of  electronics there's a technician in the  room and the plug on her head has a big  amplifier that's attached to it in order  to amplify these signals and so these  are things that have to be overcome to  make a practical everyday system so one  of the very important steps forward is  to be able to implant the electronics  inside the body so here is Matt with his  device attached just to give you a sense  of the size of this attached electronics  and here's what we're aiming to do it's  from the movie and you can see this  small flat object this would be  implanted inside the head and this is  something that I've been working with my  colleagues at the vCenter in Geneva  which is a translational neuro  Technology Center using industrial  processes funded very generously by hon  George Lisa and his philanthropy to make  this hundred channel amplifiers that can  actually transmit more than a hundred  channels at very high bandwidth out of  the body so this is a major breakthrough  it's going to take a few years for to be  realized but it's coming now  so the translation to humans is really a  very complex and challenging process as  I said it takes the integration of many  disciplines we need a neural interface  that will allow us to sample large  numbers of neurons and will be  long-lasting will last in the brain for  a lifetime  we need a implantable processors and the  implantable processors as I said are  coming soon we hope to have them  available for humans in two or three  years we have to decode this information  and we can admit that the kind of  movements either cursor movement or  robotic movements are not typical of  everyday movements so we need better  understanding of the code in the brain  in order for us to be able to produce  realistic naturalistic Lubin's we have a  number of useful devices to connect  those two and I think we can be very  encouraged that we can connect the  computer or robotic arms or hands or  even back to the body there's even a  group trying to attach the brain signals  to the spinal cord to restore locomotion  so the future is very exciting now  lastly the neuro tech we can see has the  potential for many many benefits for  people with paralysis and people with  other disorders as well we can see that  restoration of function is possible  although it has room for improvement we  can eventually create independence so  people who are paralyzed are not  dependent on others for everyday life  and since they are independent we can  reduce healthcare costs so that these so  that they are not so dependent on a  healthcare system and very expensive  care and finally one of the things  that's very important to consider when  we start recording from or stimulating  the human brain is the ethical  considerations of course we need to make  sure that these kinds of devices are  accessible to all there are very  expensive devices and we have to find  ways to make them affordable we are now  capping into the brain so there is a  privacy issue  their minds how do we protect people  from not having their thoughts broadcast  to others and and can we in fact use  these kinds of systems to evolve kinds  of mind control things we have to think  about we also have to be careful that  people don't make claims about what can  be done with these devices that are not  true this is certainly a  misrepresentation of what you could do  is an ethical challenge and lastly a  challenge that will come in the future  is augmentation what happens if we can  alter the body and provide function that  we ordinarily can't have so with that  I'd like to say that all of this effort  requires a very large team my colleagues  at Brown is the VA hospital Mass General  Case Western Stanford and that the  vCenter are all part of a very large  team who have been working together and  it made all this effort possible but  with that I thank you very much for your  time  Thank You dr. donahoo for that excellent  and engaging talk please remember that  our speaker will follow up any questions  about his presentation by email coming  up next  join our first presentation in the  clinical updates track as we hear from  dr. wolf Julian Newman will present for  its artificial intelligence based  adaptive deep brain stimulation and  movement disorders please join us there
 

